- An append-only file turns the restart from a reset into a non-event
  for the budget. Without one, Redis came back without {{$n.Lost}} of the
  key's spend and the key spent its budget a second time (overshoot
  {{$n.Overshoot}}). With either fsync policy Redis forgot {{$e.Lost}}, and the
  overshoot at the end ({{$e.Overshoot}}) is what the outage spent on grace
  ({{$e.OutageSpent}}, bound {{$e.OutageBound}}): the restart added nothing.
  `everysec` hands each write to the kernel before Redis replies and
  fsyncs once a second, so a killed Redis process, which is what this
  schedule's SIGKILL is, loses nothing it acknowledged: the bytes are in
  the kernel's page cache and the restarted Redis reads them back. What
  `everysec` risks is a crash of the machine or its kernel, which can
  lose the last second or so of writes, and writes Redis holds back while
  an earlier fsync is late (its delayed-fsync count). A container kill
  produces neither, so the one-second term in the bound was never
  exercised: the hypothesis held without being tested at its edge.
- How long the fleet stays degraded after a restart is set by two retry
  timers, not by loading the file. Redis replayed its append-only file
  (which held {{$e.AOFSize}} by the end of the run, less at the restart) in
  {{$e.AOFLoaded}}, at the end of the {{$e.Ready}} from `docker start` to ready, and no
  replica's call landed in that window, so none read a `LOADING` reply
  (the integration test is what covers that path). Every mode, the one
  without a file included, then stayed degraded for about two seconds
  ({{$n.Degraded}}, {{$e.Degraded}}, {{$a.Degraded}}), and the replicas went on logging the
  outage's DNS error after Redis was ready. After enough failed dials
  go-redis's connection pool stops dialling, returns its last dial error
  to every caller and retries once a second in the background; the
  gateway's limiter, budget and cache each probe Redis once per one-second
  retry interval; the two waits add up. The
  cache is bypassed until its probe succeeds, which is why the hit rate
  of repeats after the restart was {{$e.RepeatHitRate}} with `everysec` rather than
  the 95% expected: the entries had survived in Redis, but the replicas
  were not asking yet. The hypothesis assumed the replicas would use
  Redis again as soon as it answered.
- `always` costs about {{$ab.CostTotalP50.Point}} ms at p50 and {{$ab.CostTotalP99.Point}} ms at p99 on the
  non-streaming total: with `appendfsync always` Redis writes and fsyncs
  the file before it replies, and each request's limiter and budget calls
  wait for that, here on the container's overlay filesystem, far past the
  0.2 ms hoped for at p99. Redis counts delayed fsyncs only under
  `everysec`; under `always` a slow disk shows up only as this wait.
- `everysec`'s latency hypothesis is judged "no" by the check fixed
  before the run, on its streaming TTFT costs ({{$eb.CostTTFTP50.Point}} ms at p50, {{$eb.CostTTFTP99.Point}} ms
