## Outside reference points

Everything above compares Switchboard with itself: a direct call, an
earlier commit, a baseline built here. This section sets three of the
headline results beside the nearest published figure for the same question.
None of the outside figures was re-run here, and none shares a protocol
with the run it sits beside, so each is a reference point and not a
comparison: nothing in this file supports "faster than" or "better than"
another project. Sources were read on 2026-11-24.

- **Q1, gateway overhead, beside LiteLLM's published benchmark.** LiteLLM's
  documentation ([Benchmarks](https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/benchmarks))
  reports the proxy's own overhead against a mock OpenAI endpoint under
  Locust (1,000 users), read from its `x-litellm-overhead-duration-ms`
  response header: 2 ms median, 8 ms p95 and 13 ms p99 on four instances
  (4 vCPUs and 8 GB each) at 1,170 requests per second, and 12 ms median
  and 43 ms p99 on two instances at 1,036.{{with .Q1}}{{with .Breakdown}}{{with .Row "none"}} Passthrough here adds {{.TotalP50.Point}} ms
  at p50 and {{.TotalP99.Point}} ms at p99 at {{.Rate}} requests per second{{end}}{{with .Row "tracing"}}, and
  {{.TotalP50.Point}} and {{.TotalP99.Point}} ms with {{.Features}} on{{end}}.{{end}}{{end}}
  Where this is unfair: the load here is about a sixth of theirs and never
  near one process's capacity, on one gateway process on a shared 4-CPU
  machine; LiteLLM's figure is time measured inside the proxy and this
  one is a difference of two client-side runs; and their proxy ran with
  PostgreSQL attached, where the passthrough row has every feature off.
  A LiteLLM arm of `make bench` against the same fake provider on the
  same machine would make it a comparison; it is under To measure.
- **Q3, false hits at a default threshold, beside MeanCache's measurement
  of GPTCache.** Gill et al., "MeanCache: User-Centric Semantic Caching
  for LLM Web Services" ([arXiv:2403.02694](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02694),
  Table 1 of v3) ran GPTCache with Albert embeddings at its cosine
  threshold of 0.7 on GPTCache's own 1,000-query set (30% repeats) and
  measured a hit precision of 0.52 at recall 0.85, so about half of the
  served hits were answers to a different question; their own cache
  reached 0.72. The "at a guessed threshold" table above is the same
  finding on harder data: at 0.90 to 0.97 most adversarial non-paraphrases
  hit.{{with .Q3}}{{with .Sweep}}{{if .Chosen.Model}} The threshold the eval chose ({{.ChosenThreshold}}) gives up nearly all
  of the hit rate to get there (hit / false-hit: {{.ChosenHitFalse}}).{{end}}{{end}}{{end}}
  Where this is unfair: the metrics differ (their precision is over a
  query stream against a filled cache; the false-hit rate here is per
  pair over negatives, a floor for a filled cache), the embedders differ,
  and PAWS and the GSM8K perturbations are built to be near-duplicates
  with a different answer, where their set is ordinary traffic with
  repeats. GPTCache was not run on these pair sets.
- **Q5, the router against a random mix, beside RouterBench.** Hu et al.,
  "RouterBench: A Benchmark for Multi-LLM Routing System"
  ([arXiv:2403.12031](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12031), March 2024, v2)
  define the Zero router as the non-decreasing convex hull of the
  candidate models, reached by mixing them at random, and call a router
  significant only if it beats it. That is the hull used above (it was
  added here after the first run, without knowing the paper's name for
  it). Their KNN and MLP routers, which predict per-model quality from
  the prompt as the router here does, did not significantly outperform
  the Zero router overall (section 5.3: above it on MMLU and Winogrande,
  level on Hellaswag and GSM8K, below on ARC-Challenge and MBPP).{{with .Q5}}{{with .Findings}}{{with $r := index .Variant "router"}} The
  result here agrees: {{$r.AUCHullGain}} points over the hull averaged across the
  cost range ({{$r.AUCHullGainCI}}).{{end}}{{end}}{{end}}
  Where this is unfair: RouterBench routes between hosted and open
  models priced in dollars (over 405k recorded outcomes) and scores the
  area under the cost-quality curve; this eval has three sizes of one
  local model family, compute as cost, and 600 questions from two
  datasets. The
  agreement is in the direction of the finding, not in any number.

## Known problems

Measured weaknesses and cases the runs could not reach, each with what was
observed.
{{with .M11}}{{with .Main}}
- **Pending budget spend dies with its replica.** Costs a replica settles
  for requests it admitted on grace are kept in its memory until Redis
  answers again, and a process killed before then takes them with it.
  Since M12 this is only grace spend: the cost of a request held on Redis
  and settled during the outage keeps its hold's id, and if the process
  dies the hold expires and is charged.{{with .Example "killed pending"}} M11's shortest schedule
  (seed {{.Seed}}): {{.Line}}.{{end}}{{with $.M12}}{{with .Sim}} In M12's re-run the class is still found
  on {{(index .Classes 0).Seeds}} seeds.{{end}}{{end}}{{with $.M8}} In M8 the replica killed during an
  outage took {{.LostBefore}} with it before the grace fix and {{.LostAfter}} after it (its
  allowance was already spent).{{end}}
- **A request admitted on grace and in flight when its process dies is
  lost.** Found by M11. The cost is neither pending spend nor a hold on
  Redis that belongs to it: only the dead process's grace allowance knew
  it.{{with .Example "other"}} M11's shortest schedule (seed {{.Seed}}): {{.Line}}.{{end}}{{with $.M12}}{{with .Sim}}{{with .Main.Example "other"}}
  M12's (seed {{.Seed}}): {{.Line}}; the orphaned hold is charged, and the
  loss is what the request cost above it.{{end}}{{end}}{{end}}
  `degrade.go`'s loss bound (what the allowance admitted) covers the
  amount. A lost reply on a reserve reaches the same state without an
  outage; since M12 the orphaned hold that reply left on Redis expires and
  is charged at the request's estimate, so on that path the store
  over-counts instead (the estimate and the grace request's cost are both
  charged), and the outage path (store unreachable, reserve, kill:
  `TestSimSchedules`) still loses the request.
- **An expired hold is charged at its worst case.** Since M12 a hold
  whose replica died while Redis was up is charged as spent at its held
  amount (input estimate plus `max_tokens`) when it expires, and a late
  settle corrects it. Nothing is lost, but the key is over-counted by
  the hold's margin over what the provider billed{{with $.M12}}{{with .After}}: {{.OverCountOverBilled}}× the bill
  for the {{.InFlight}} streams cut off in M12's run{{end}}{{end}}, which errs on refusing.
  The tombstones of a total budget never expire (one hash field per hold
  that lapsed unsettled). A request that costs more than its hold (a low
  input estimate) still loses the difference when its replica dies{{with $.M12}}{{with .Sim}}: the
  "expired hold" class of M12's simulator re-run, {{(index .Classes 1).Seeds}} seeds{{end}}{{end}}.
  An expiry is charged by the next script that touches the key and
  window, so a window nobody reads again shows its last expired holds
  only when it is next read.
- **A settle that timed out after Redis applied it is charged twice.**
  The replica cannot tell it from one that never arrived, so it keeps the
  cost as pending and charges it again: an over-count, never a miss. In
  M11, {{.OverCounted}} of {{.Seeds}} seeds ended with `spent` above billed, {{.OverCountedLostReply}} of
  them after a lost reply; the two-step schedule (a reserve, then a settle
  whose reply is lost) is a unit test (`TestSimSchedules`).
{{end}}{{else}}{{with .M8}}
- **Pending budget spend dies with its replica.** Costs a replica settles
  on grace are kept in its memory until Redis answers again. In M8 the
  replica killed during an outage took {{.LostBefore}} with it before the grace
  fix and {{.LostAfter}} after it (its allowance was already spent). The loss is
  at most one allowance ({{.Grace}}) plus the requests it had reserved on Redis
  before the outage and settled during it; a replica killed late in its
  first outage would come close.
- **A settle that timed out after Redis applied it is charged twice.**
  The replica cannot tell it from one that never arrived, so it keeps the
  cost as pending and charges it again: an over-count, never a miss. Not
  observed in M8: no reconciliation gap was negative in either run.
- **A hold whose replica died while Redis was up expires without its cost
  reaching `spent`.** The request was sent upstream; nobody settles it,
  and after the reservation TTL the hold is released. Not reached by M8,
  whose kill came during an outage (`reserved` was 0 at every snapshot);
  Q6's replica kill, with Redis up, is the case, and its reconciliation is
  not recorded.
{{else}}
- Budget reconciliation under a flapping store: not measured yet
  (`make chaos-flap`).
{{end}}{{end}}{{with .M13}}{{if eq (len .Retry) 2}}{{$slow := index .Retry 0}}{{$fast := index .Retry 1}}- **About a second degraded after Redis comes back, decided by luck.**
  A killed Redis trips go-redis's dial breaker, which then redials once
  a second in the background whatever the gateway's settings; only after
  that dial succeeds does each component's next probe (one retry interval
  later at most) find Redis. M13 measured {{$slow.AfterReady}} s after ready at the
  1 s default (mean {{$slow.MeanAfterReady}} s) and {{$fast.AfterReady}} s at 250 ms (mean {{$fast.MeanAfterReady}} s),
  three runs each: the shorter interval saves about what its shorter
  probe wait predicts, but the redial's phase spreads the runs over most
  of a second, and the default stays {{if eq .Default "1s"}}1 s{{else}}{{.Default}}{{end}} under PLAN.md's rule. M9's
  "about two seconds" was from `docker start`, not from ready. The cost
  is the cache's hits and the shared limits for that second: no wrong
  answers. Shortening it would mean giving the pool a redial that the
  gateway controls, which go-redis does not expose.
{{end}}{{else}}- **About two seconds degraded after Redis comes back.** go-redis's
  dial backoff and the gateway's one-second probe run one after the
  other, so a replica keeps using its local limits, grace and no cache for
  about two seconds after Redis is ready (M9, every mode). The cache is
  the visible cost: hits lost, not wrong answers.
{{end}}{{with .M13}}{{with .After}}- **Every Redis outage still costs a burst of slow requests at the cut.**
  The lookups that start before the first one fails each wait out the
  cache's 250 ms timeout ({{(index $.M13.Bursts 0).After.PerOutage}} slow requests an outage after M13's single
  probe), since nothing knows the store is down yet; and when go-redis's
  dial breaker has not tripped, each replica's probe waits 250 ms once a
  retry interval for the whole outage. A shorter cache timeout would
  shrink both; it was not measured.
{{end}}{{end}}- **`everysec` can lose the last second of charges to a machine crash.**
  A killed Redis process loses nothing it acknowledged (the kernel holds
  the written bytes), which is the case M9's container kill tested; a
  crash of the machine or kernel in the middle of a key's spending
  forgets up to about a second of its writes, and no run here produces
  one. With no append-only file (`REDIS_PERSIST=none`, Q6) a restart
  forgets every budget.
- **A first-token timeout cannot see a stall after the first token.**
  A provider that sends its first token and then stops holds the request
  until the provider's whole-call `timeout` (10 minutes by default) or the
  client gives up, and the breaker never hears of it. Non-streamed
  requests are not covered either: their reply arrives whole, so only the
  provider's `timeout` bounds them. Neither case is measured.
- **A `LOADING` Redis was not reached under load.** M9's append-only file
  loaded in tens of milliseconds and no replica called during it; the integration
  test shows a `LOADING` reply degrades like an unreachable store and
  recovers, but no chaos run has a Redis that loads for seconds.

